// Shifts, including the two counts that are not a shift at all.
//
// `1 << 64` is zero and `-8 >> 1` is -4 by docs/SPEC.md 2.3, and the second of
// those is what separates the arithmetic shift from the logical one -- so the
// same value appears again through `ushr`, where the sign bit does not travel.

// The second line is the counts that are past the width rather than inside it:
// `>>` smears the sign bit and everything else empties.
//
// A *negative* count is deliberately not here. docs/SPEC.md 2.3 says it traps,
// and a kernel has no stderr to trap to (docs/KERNEL.md), so it is the one
// thing on this page the two runtimes could not agree about -- and agreeing is
// what tests/kernel.sh checks these lines for.
show_shift() {
  print("shift: " + (1 << 10) + " " + ((0 - 8) >> 1) + " " + (1 << 64) + " " + ushr(0 - 8, 60));
  print("shift: " + ((0 - 1) >> 70) + " " + (1 >> 70) + " " + ult(3, 0 - 1) + " " + ult(0 - 1, 3));
} return void;
